Due to Rust's operator precedence (`&& binds tighter than ||), this is: starts_with("public") || (!starts_with("private") && !starts_with("protected")). But !starts_with("private") && !starts_with("protected") is actually true` for everything that doesn't start with private or protected — including things with no visibility modifier. So this entire expression simplifies to just !starts_with("private") && !starts_with("protected") (because if it starts with "public", then it doesn't start with "private" or "protected" either).

